ings are expressible we may not be  able to prove the properties that we  need  conversely there might be some  completely different other method  for fraga to assimilate extended fraga  that does not involve  converting circuits to formulas so  neither of these possibilities can be  ruled out  nonetheless it makes a nice way to think  about  and to motivate fraga versus extended  figure proofs  so our current state of the art just to  run through some review here  is any two frag approved systems piece  simulate each other  um likewise any two extended fraga proof  systems p simulate each other  it's clear just trivially that extended  frago systems p simulate fragrance  systems  it on the other hand it's open whether  fragus systems simulate extended  fragrance systems  it's open whether either of these  systems is polynomially bounded  uh already mentioned that extended  resolution extended fragrance systems  pieces simulate each other  it should be clear that fragrance  systems p simulate  red resolution on the other hand and  we'll see later that resolution does not  simulate fragis systems  and already mentioned that regular  resolution does not simulate resolution  so in the talks going forward in  particular the next two talks are part b  and part c and they can be watched  independently of each other  part b will discuss what's known about  separating resolution  frag and extended fragrance systems  especially using the pigeon principle  and part c will discuss resolution and  its subsystems  and how they relate to conflict  uh conflict directed clause learning  cdcl proof search  and part d will discuss yet further  approved systems  so this is the end of part a and before  you depart  let me just mention there's several  survey articles available to read um  including one that the very top one  there that's just coming out  i think approximately this month uh in  early 2021  uh by jacob nordstrom and myself  on proof complexity
 

